
What Is an Object in JavaScript?
=====================================

An **object** in JavaScript is a collection of **key-value pairs** (properties).

* The keys are strings (called property names).
* The values can be anything — including functions ,string, number, boolean, function, array, object, etc.	
						
2. Accessing Properties

**Dot notation:

console.log(person.name); // Alice

**Bracket notation (useful for dynamic keys):

console.log(person["age"]); // 30


✔ Can be created in many ways:
===================================

Object literal (most common)

let obj = { key: "value" };

Using new Object() constructor

let obj = new Object();
obj.key = "value";  // do print 

Using a class or constructor function

function Car(model) {
  this.model = model;
}
let myCar = new Car("Tesla");

🧠 Why Use Objects Instead of Other Data Types?
------------------------------------------------------

Objects group related values into one logical unit.

Object keys make your code more readable and self-documenting.
